<TopicPage slug="subjects/neuroscience/intermediate/the-action-potential">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an intermediate lesson in Neuroscience. It assumes the foundational lesson The Neuron and
    the intermediate Neurons and Synapses — that lesson ended at the threshold, with a postsynaptic
    cell summing its inputs, and this one is what happens next. No physics beyond "opposite charges
    attract" and no equation beyond arithmetic is required; the membrane is treated as a battery and
    built up from there.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain the resting potential as a pump-maintained ion gradient, and why the membrane rests near minus 70 mV;</li>
    <li>narrate the spike phase by phase — threshold, depolarisation, repolarisation, hyperpolarisation — as voltage-gated channels opening and closing;</li>
    <li>state the all-or-none principle and its consequences: frequency coding, the refractory periods, one-way travel;</li>
    <li>explain propagation and saltatory conduction, and why myelin is both faster and cheaper;</li>
    <li>describe how toxins and anaesthetics attack channels, and why the channels are medicine's favourite addresses.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The last lesson ended at the threshold: enough excitatory votes, and the postsynaptic neuron
    fires. What fires is the <strong>action potential</strong> — a millisecond electrical spike,
    essentially identical in every neuron in every animal, the brain's universal unit of long-range
    signalling. Its mechanism was decoded between 1949 and 1952 by Alan Hodgkin and Andrew Huxley,
    using the squid's giant axon (a fibre half a millimetre thick, evolved for escape speed and,
    luckily for science, big enough to thread wires into), a voltage-clamp apparatus, and
    arithmetic of startling predictive power. Their five 1952 papers are among biology's greatest,
    and they earned the 1963 Nobel Prize. This lesson rebuilds the whole story: the charged resting
    state, the spike itself, the all-or-none principle and its coding consequences, propagation
    down the axon, and the channels as the field's favourite drug and toxin targets.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the spike, phase by phase — and the pump that resets it" widget="equationStep" config={ap2Cfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the five panels are the spike's timeline, and each one names the
    channel machinery responsible. Panel 1 is the cocked spring — the pump holding minus 70 mV
    against the gradient; panel 2 is the decision point, where subthreshold signals die but
    threshold snaps everything open; panel 3 is the positive-feedback flood to plus 40 mV; panel 4
    is the automatic brake, sodium inactivating and potassium restoring; panel 5 is the wave
    renewing itself down the axon, myelin making it jump. Keep one thread in view: every phase is
    the previous phase's voltage opening the next channel.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the action potential is a millisecond spike, universal across animals,
    decoded on the squid's giant axon. The next section starts before the spike — with the charge it
    is spent from.
  </p>

  <h2>The resting potential: a charged battery</h2>
  <p>
    Every spike is spent out of a charge that must first be built. The neuron's membrane is a lipid
    sheet dotted with channels and pumps; inside sits a fluid loaded with potassium (K⁺) and
    negatively charged proteins, outside a seawater-like fluid loaded with sodium (Na⁺) and
    chloride. The <strong>sodium–potassium pump</strong> maintains the imbalance by shuffling 3 Na⁺
    out for every 2 K⁺ in — an active, ATP-burning exchange against the gradient, the reason the
    brain devours a fifth of your resting energy. Meanwhile <strong>potassium leak channels</strong>
    (open at rest, sodium's equivalents mostly closed) let K⁺ diffuse out down its gradient, leaving
    behind unpaired negative charge; the electrical pull-back grows until it exactly balances the
    chemical push-out, at potassium's equilibrium near −90 mV. The real membrane rests at
    <strong>about −70 mV</strong> — a compromise between K⁺ (dominant) and a small Na⁺ leak. That
    number is a genuine battery: a five-volt-per-millimetre field across a membrane only five
    nanometres thick, one of the sturdiest insulators known, sustained by every neuron simultaneously.
    The resting potential is not dormancy — it is the cocked spring from which all signalling is
    released.
  </p>

  <h2>The spike, phase by phase</h2>
  <p>
    Inputs from synapses nudge the membrane voltage up and down; if the sum at the axon initial
    segment crosses <strong>threshold (about −55 mV)</strong>, the spike ignites, and the ignition
    is gated by proteins. <strong>Voltage-gated sodium channels</strong> — closed at rest, sprung
    open by depolarisation — begin admitting Na⁺; the incoming positive charge depolarises the
    membrane further, opening more channels, which depolarises further: a positive-feedback
    explosion. Voltage rockets from −55 mV to about <strong>+40 mV in under a millisecond</strong>,
    approaching sodium's equilibrium (the membrane never quite reaches it because potassium conductance
    is already rising). Then the automatic brakes fire: sodium channels <strong>inactivate</strong>
    — a built-in molecular plug (the inactivation "ball") swings shut independently of voltage — and
    slower <strong>voltage-gated potassium channels</strong>, opened by the same depolarisation,
    now dominate, letting K⁺ rush out. The membrane repolarises, overshoots the resting value
    (the <strong>afterhyperpolarisation</strong>, because K⁺ channels close gradually), and the
    pump plus leak currents restore −70 mV. Total elapsed time: one to two milliseconds. The
    elegance is that no single step needs supervision — each voltage opens the channel whose current
    creates the next voltage, a chain of feedbacks Hodgkin and Huxley captured in four coupled
    equations that predict, not describe: plug their model's numbers into conduction-velocity
    formulas and you get the real velocity of a squid axon. All of it runs on gradients the pump
    built — so, strictly, <em>the pump fires every spike</em>, in slow motion, and the channels
    merely release it fast.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the spike does not consume or reverse the cell's ion gradients
  in any dramatic way. The number of ions crossing per spike is minuscule compared with the pool —
  a neuron could fire spikes for a while on its gradients even with the pump poisoned — but not
  indefinitely, and restoring the gradients is where the ongoing metabolic bill is paid.</p>

  <h2>All-or-none and frequency coding</h2>
  <p>
    The spike's deepest property is that it is <strong>all-or-none</strong>. Because the feedback is
    regenerative, any suprathreshold input produces the same stereotyped spike; subthreshold inputs
    produce nothing (they decay passively along the membrane, which the next lesson exploits).
    There are no half-spikes, and no spike-to-spike variation in shape to read meaning from. The
    consequence is that a single neuron encodes intensity not by spike size but by spike
    <em>rate and timing</em> — frequency coding: a brighter light, a heavier weight, a louder tone
    makes the afferent fire faster, not harder. Rate rises roughly logarithmically with stimulus
    intensity across senses (Weber–Fechner territory), and precise spike <em>timing</em> carries
    extra information where circuits use it. Two further consequences follow from the refractory
    machinery. The <strong>absolute refractory period</strong> — the millisecond or so while sodium
    channels are inactivated — sets a hard ceiling on firing rate (the theoretical maximum is about
    1,000 Hz; sustained cortical firing rarely exceeds a few hundred) and forces <strong>one-way
    travel</strong>: the segment behind a spike cannot re-fire, so the wave never doubles back on
    itself. The <strong>relative refractory period</strong> — the lingering afterhyperpolarisation —
    makes immediate re-firing harder, shaping how inputs integrate in time. Information in the
    nervous system, then, rides on a digital code (uniform spikes) whose analogue content is in the
    statistics: rate, rhythm, synchrony.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> a stronger stimulus produces a bigger action potential. The spike is stereotyped — same amplitude and shape whether the stimulus barely cleared threshold or doubled it; intensity is encoded in how often spikes arrive, not how tall they are. The only way for a neuron to shout is faster, not louder.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the spike is all-or-none, so a single neuron encodes intensity in rate
    and timing, with the refractory machinery capping the rate and forcing one-way travel. The next
    section follows the wave as it renews itself down the axon — and meets the insulation that makes
    it fast.
  </p>

  <h2>Propagation: the self-renewing wave and its insulation</h2>
  <p>
    A spike is not a signal that <em>travels</em> like a ball thrown down a corridor — it is a wave
    that <em>re-erupts</em> segment by segment. The depolarisation at the active patch spreads
    passively (the way a battery's field reaches through a wire) to the adjacent patch of membrane;
    if that patch crosses threshold, its own sodium channels ignite, regenerating the spike at full
    amplitude there. Because each segment renews the signal to full height, the action potential
    does not attenuate — it arrives at the axon terminal exactly as tall as it left the trigger
    zone, whether the axon is a millimetre or a metre long. Unmyelinated, though, this is slow and
    expensive: conduction in thin naked axons runs at 0.5–2 m/s, and every millimetre re-fires.
    Vertebrates solved this with <strong>myelin</strong> — glial membranes wrapped in tight spiral
    around the axon, insulating it so the electrical disturbance leaks almost nothing and spreads
    fast, while the spike regenerates only at bare gaps, the <strong>nodes of Ranvier</strong>
    (densely loaded with sodium channels), every millimetre or so. This <strong>saltatory
    conduction</strong> — leaping, from node to node — lifts speeds to <strong>120 m/s</strong> in
    the fastest human fibres, cuts the metabolic cost roughly tenfold for a given speed, and lets
    the brain pack many more, thinner fibres into the same skull. The clinical proof of the design
    is multiple sclerosis, where immune attack strips myelin: spikes leak out, slow, or fail
    entirely across the denuded stretches — producing the disease's unpredictable patchwork of
    sensory, motor and visual deficits. (Myelin is why squid axons are giant: invertebrates never
    evolved the wrapper, so they bought speed with brute diameter — and gave neurophysiology its
    best preparation in the process.)
  </p>
  <p>
    The speed numbers deserve a concrete feel, because they explain how bodies are built. A stretch
    reflex — tap the kneecap, and the leg extends — runs a two-neuron loop from thigh to spinal
    cord and back, about a metre of round trip: on thick myelinated fibres at ~100 m/s the whole
    reflex completes in tens of milliseconds, fast enough to work before you consciously notice
    the tap. Compare the same distance on unmyelinated pain fibres (thin, in the 1 m/s class):
    that is why the sharp "first pain" of a stubbed toe arrives well before the slow, spreading
    ache — the two sensations ride fibres whose conduction velocities differ a hundredfold. At the
    other extreme, a giraffe's motor axons to its hind legs run several metres, a blue whale's can
    run fifteen; without myelinated, 100-m/s-class conduction, an animal that size would be
    coordinating its hindquarters on multi-second delays. That design constraint made myelin one
    of the decisive inventions of vertebrate evolution — and its loss, in multiple sclerosis, a
    uniquely disabling failure mode.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> myelin sheathes the nodes of Ranvier. It is the reverse: myelin wraps the stretches between nodes, and the nodes are the bare, sodium-channel-rich gaps where the spike regenerates. The two parts fail differently — stripped internodes leak charge, blocked nodes have nowhere to re-ignite — which is one reason demyelinating disease produces such patchy deficits.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the wave re-erupts segment by segment, and myelin buys a hundredfold
    speed by forcing it to jump — which is also why stripped myelin, as in multiple sclerosis, is so
    disabling. The next section turns from the design to its enemies: the toxins and drugs that
    attack the channels directly.
  </p>

  <h2>Channels as targets: toxins and anaesthetics</h2>
  <p>
    Because the spike is channel machinery, the channels are where chemistry attacks it — and
    evolution found them first. <strong>Tetrodotoxin</strong>, the pufferfish poison (fugu chefs
    license around it), binds the sodium channel's outer pore and blocks it: a few milligrams
    paralyse and kill by silencing every spike in the body, breathing last. Scorpion toxins hold
    channels open or lock them shut; batrachotoxin (poison frogs) jams them permanently open. Each
    toxin's exquisite specificity made it a research tool — different toxins dissected activation,
    inactivation and permeation, the dissection that produced the modern channel atlas. Medicine
    aims more gently. <strong>Local anaesthetics</strong> (lidocaine, novocaine) enter the channel
    pore from the inside and block it, with a crucial twist: they bind preferentially to the
    open or inactivated state, so rapidly firing fibres are silenced first — <em>use-dependent</em>
    block, a self-selecting strike on the busiest signalling. Anticonvulsants (carbamazepine,
    phenytoin) exploit the same state-dependence to quiet the runaway firing of seizures while
    leaving ordinary traffic mostly intact, and tetrodotoxin-inspired blockers are being developed
    as non-opioid painkillers. The lesson generalises: nearly every channel subtype discovered
    (sodium 1.1 through 1.9, the potassium, calcium and chloride families) has become a
    pharmaceutical address, and "channelopathy" is now a recognised disease category — inherited
    epilepsy, migraine, cardiac arrhythmia and pain syndromes traced to single amino-acid changes
    in these gates.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Potassium leak alone would settle the membrane near minus 90 mV. Why does the neuron rest nearer minus 70 mV instead?</li>
    <li>Which machinery makes an immediate second spike impossible, and what two useful properties does that buy the neuron?</li>
    <li>Why is myelinated conduction both faster and metabolically cheaper than conduction in a naked axon?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Potassium leak dominates but is not the only leak: a small sodium
    leak pulls the resting voltage toward sodium's equilibrium, and about minus 70 mV is the
    compromise between the two — still a genuine battery, maintained by the pump. (2) Sodium channel
    inactivation — the absolute refractory period. It caps firing rate at roughly a thousand per
    second and forces one-way travel, since the segment behind a spike cannot re-fire while
    inactivated. (3) Myelin insulates the internodes so the electrical disturbance leaks almost
    nothing and spreads fast, and the spike regenerates only at the nodes — about tenfold less
    metabolic work for the same distance, and far greater speed, because most of the axon never has
    to re-ignite.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The resting potential is a pump-maintained ion gradient; the membrane is a charged battery, not a dormant wire.</li>
    <li>Threshold triggers a positive-feedback flood of sodium; inactivation and potassium efflux then restore the resting state — no supervisor needed.</li>
    <li>The spike is all-or-none: intensity is encoded in firing rate and timing, never in spike height.</li>
    <li>Refractory machinery caps the rate and forces one-way travel; the maximum is about a thousand spikes per second.</li>
    <li>Propagation renews the signal segment by segment, so a spike arrives as tall as it left, whatever the axon's length.</li>
    <li>Myelin forces saltatory conduction — about 120 m/s at a tenth of the metabolic cost — and its loss is multiple sclerosis.</li>
    <li>Channels are the drug and toxin targets of the nervous system; local anaesthetics silence the busiest fibres first.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The sibling lesson Neurons and Synapses sits upstream — how inputs sum toward the threshold this
    lesson crossed; Movement and the Motor System shows the spikes arriving at muscle; and
