    Perception puts frequency coding to work turning light into perception.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Four refinements for the expert. <strong>The Hodgkin–Huxley model's real achievement</strong>
    was methodology: the voltage clamp (invented with Kenneth Cole) held voltage fixed so currents
    could be measured, separating the membrane's conductances into a sodium arm, a potassium arm and
    a leak — and the resulting equations predicted the shape, threshold and conduction velocity of
    real spikes years before channels were even hypothesised as molecules. Their m, n and h gating
    variables remain, sixty years on, the starting point of every computational-neuroscience course.
    <strong>The energy ledger</strong>: the Na⁺/K⁺ pump and glutamate recycling consume the
    majority of the brain's ~20 W, so evolution prices spikes steeply — which explains the sparse,
    efficient firing codes real circuits use (most cortical neurons fire at a few spikes per second,
    not hundreds) and why anoxic insults (stroke, cardiac arrest) kill neurons within minutes: the
    gradients run down, channels leak open, and the resulting flood of depolarisation and
    glutamate-triggered calcium is the mechanism of ischaemic cell death.
    <strong>Threshold is fuzzy</strong>: the "−55 mV" is a pedagogical constant; real threshold
    varies with input history (recent inactivation, membrane time constant, channel density along
    the axon initial segment), which is why real neurons are noisy threshold-crossers and why
    stochastic channel noise is a live research topic rather than a footnote. And <strong>the spike
    is not the only long-range signal</strong>: dendrites support local regenerative events,
    astrocytes propagate calcium waves, and ephaptic (field-effect) coupling measurably synchronises
    neighbouring axons — but for the brain's own long-haul traffic, the all-or-none sodium spike,
    regenerated node by node, remains the only technology evolution shipped at scale, which is why
    understanding it is understanding the nervous system's single shared dialect.
  </p>
